course: english
lesson: 40
title: Openings that hook the reader
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The first line of a piece of writing is a fishing hook: its job is to catch the reader and make them want the next line, and the next.
explain: "Once upon a time there was a girl called Ella who lived in a house" tells us little and asks nothing, while "'Run!' shouted Max. Behind him, the gate creaked open" makes the whole class gasp, because we want to know what is behind the gate.
explain: Writers have a toolbox of hooks: speech, action, a sound ("CRASH! The vase lay in pieces"), a question ("Have you ever wondered why cats purr?"), a strange detail, or starting in the middle of the action and explaining how we got there later.
explain: A hook must belong to the story: an explosion in line one that is never mentioned again, or a random "SKIBIDI!" shouted by a dog, grabs attention for a second and then feels like a trick.
explain: Openings and endings are partners: if the opening hooks with a problem, the ending should solve it, which is why "and then I woke up and it was all a dream" makes teachers sigh, as it throws the whole story away.
fact: A hook is an opening that makes the reader want to read on; many readers decide in the first few lines whether to keep going.
fact: Common hooks are dialogue, action, a sound word, a question, a surprising fact and a strange detail.
fact: Starting in the middle of the action means the reader is hooked first and learns the background later through small hints.
fact: After a dialogue or action opening, the next sentences should quickly show who and where.
fact: Setting openings can build mood, but a long list of things in the sky and on the ground slows a story down.
fact: Question openings work in stories and in information texts.
fact: A cliché is a phrase used so often that it has lost its power, such as "It was a dark and stormy night".
fact: Fairy-tale openings like "Once upon a time" suit fairy tales, but other stories usually need a sharper first line.
fact: An ending where everything was a dream is a well-known cliché that makes the story feel as if it did not matter.
fact: A good ending answers the problem set up at the start.
fact: In Liverpool "boss" means great; in the East Midlands "me duck" is a friendly way to address someone.
fact: Gen Alpha children shout "skibidi" as a funny word that means nothing in particular.
exercise: Ella's story begins "Once upon a time there was a girl called Ella who lived in a house." Her teacher writes "Hook me in!" What is missing? | Something that makes us want to read on | A longer list of facts about the house | The date and the weather on that day | Her full name, age and address
exercise: The teacher writes "'Run!' shouted Max. Behind him, the gate creaked open." Which kind of hook is it? | Dialogue and action | A list of facts | A fairy-tale opening | A question to the reader
exercise: Year 5: "Have you ever wondered why cats purr?" Which kind of hook is this? | A question | A sound | A dialogue | A cliché
exercise: Liam from Liverpool boasts "Me openin's well boss, there's an explosion in the first line, like!" What should he check? | That the explosion matters to the rest of the story | That the explosion is as loud as he can make it | That there are more explosions later on | That "boss" is spelled with a capital
exercise: A Year 4 story opens with five lines about the sky, the trees, the grass and the clouds. What is the problem? | Nothing happens, so the story starts slowly | Weather can never be described in a story | Five lines is the wrong number for a start | Trees and clouds are too hard to spell
exercise: Mia, 8, starts with "SKIBIDI! said the dog." Her teacher asks "Does it fit your story?" Why? | A shock opening must link to the story | Dogs are not allowed to talk in stories at school | "Skibidi" must always be written in lower case | The teacher has never heard of the word
exercise: A Year 6 teacher says "Start in the middle of the action." What comes later? | How the characters got there | The title of the whole story | The very first opening line | Nothing at all, ever again
exercise: Grandad in Nottingham says "In my day, every story began 'It was a dark and stormy night', me duck." What is the problem with that line now? | It has been used so much it is a cliché | Stormy nights are not allowed in stories now | It is too short to work as an opening | It is spelled wrongly in his version
exercise: Josh ends "Then I woke up and it was all a dream." Why does his teacher sigh? | It makes the whole story not matter | Dreams are too scary for school stories | It uses too many words in one line | Endings should be at the start
exercise: Year 4: "CRASH! The vase lay in pieces." What kind of hook is this? | A sound | A question | A fact | A list
exercise: After "'Run!' shouted Max", what should the next sentence or two show? | Where Max is and why he runs | Max's favourite food and pets | What Max will do next year | A list of every road in the town
exercise: Which is the best opening for a report on bees? | Without bees, many fruits would not grow. | This is my report about bees, which are insects. | Bees. | I am going to write about bees now, so read on.
exercise: Which opening starts in the middle of the action? | The rope snapped and Kit fell. | Kit was born in a small town. | Once there was a girl called Kit. | This story is about Kit.
exercise: What is a cliché? | A phrase used so much it has lost its power | A word that is almost always spelled wrongly | A clever new phrase that nobody has used yet | A sound word like "crash" or "bang"
exercise: When does "Once upon a time" work well? | In a fairy tale | In a news report | In a science report | In instructions for a game
exercise: How should a story's ending match its hook? | It should solve the problem the opening set up | It should start a brand new problem | It should repeat the first line word for word | It should not mention anything from the opening
test: Your mate goes "first line don't matter, the teacher reads it all anyway, innit." What is the truth? | Real readers decide early whether to go on | Teachers never read past the first line of anything | The first line is the least important part of a story | Only the title matters to readers, never the words
test: ★★★ Which opening makes the reader ask the most questions? | Nobody had opened the red door in fifty years, until today. | It was a sunny day and I went to the park with my mum. | My name is Sam and I am nine and I like dogs a lot. | This is a story about a door that was in a house.
test: In a cartoon, a book's first page is just "Blah blah blah, the end." What would a writing teacher say? | It needs a hook and a real story | It is perfect because it is short | It needs more blahs to be funny | Books should only have one page
test: What would happen if every story in your class began "Once upon a time"? | The openings would all feel the same and dull | Every single story would turn into a fairy tale | The stories would hook every reader much more | Nothing, because openings never matter at all
test: A girl in Boston says "That first line is wicked good." ("Wicked" means very in New England.) Which line might she mean? | The phone rang at midnight, but nobody in the house owned a phone. | I woke up and had my breakfast and went to school. | This is my story. | Once upon a time there was a boy.
test: ★★★ Ava writes "Splash! Freezing water filled my wellies." Her ending is "I got home and had a bath." Which ending matches the opening better? | I never went near that river in wellies again. | And then I woke up and it was all a dream. | The end, thanks for reading my story about stuff. | Then we had chips for tea.
test: Your brother, dead sarcastic: "Wow, 'It was a dark and stormy night'. So original." What is he hinting? | The opening is a cliché | The opening is very fresh | The opening is too long | The opening is a question
test: A lad in Cork says "Yer man's story starts with a dragon and never has one again." ("Yer man" means that fella.) What is wrong? | The hook doesn't belong to the story | Dragons are banned in all school stories | Stories need at least two dragons to work | Nothing, any hook at all is fine to use
test: ★★★ Which non-fiction opening is a surprising fact? | A blue whale's heart is as big as a small car. | Whales are large animals that live in the sea. | I like whales very much, they are my favourite. | Here is my report on whales.
test: A Gen Z cousin says "Starting with the plot twist is lowkey iconic." ("Lowkey" means a bit secretly, "iconic" means amazing.) What technique is that? | Starting in the middle of the action | Starting with a fairy-tale line | Starting with a long list of the weather | Starting with "Dear diary" and the date
test: Which opening uses speech as a hook? | "Don't open that box," whispered Gran. | The box was on the table in Gran's kitchen. | It was a Tuesday. | Gran had a box.
exam: Identify the principal function of the opening sentence of a narrative. | To engage the reader so that they continue reading | To list all the characters who subsequently appear | To state the conclusion of the narrative in advance | To provide the date of the composition
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the phrase "It was a dark and stormy night" in contemporary teaching? | A cliché, owing to excessive use | A model opening for all narratives | A question designed to engage the reader | An example of the passive voice
exam: Select the opening consistent with the technique of commencing in the middle of the action. | The ice cracked beneath Ola's feet. | Ola was born in Norway in a cold town. | This narrative concerns a girl named Ola. | Once upon a time there lived a girl.
exam: Ascertain the principal objection to an ending in which events are revealed to have been a dream. | It renders the preceding events without consequence | It is excessively long and tedious in all instances | It contains no punctuation of any kind whatsoever | It is prohibited in all examinations
